<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Tajik SSR</h1><p class="st-meta">1929 CE – 1991 CE · capital Stalinabad</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Soviet republic of the Tajiks, the poorest in the Soviet Union, whose capital Stalinabad grew into modern Dushanbe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In October 1929 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tajik-assr">Tajik ASSR</a> was separated from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/uzbek-ssr">Uzbek SSR</a> and upgraded to a union republic, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajiks">Tajik</a> SSR, with the addition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khujand">Khujand</a> district. The capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dushanbe">Dushanbe</a> was named Stalinabad from 1929 to 1961.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic was reshaped by collectivisation and forced resettlement of mountain populations to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> fields of the south. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakhsh-river">Vakhsh</a> valley was irrigated, and huge projects such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurek-dam">Nurek dam</a> on the Vakhsh and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tursunzoda">Tursunzoda</a> aluminium smelter followed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajiks">Tajik</a> literature flourished under writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadriddin-ayni">Sadriddin Ayni</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamir-mountains">Pamirs</a> formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorno-badakhshan-autonomous-region">Gorno-Badakhshan Autonomous Oblast</a>. Leaders were mostly drawn from the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khujand">Khujand</a> region.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Riots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dushanbe">Dushanbe</a> in 1990 foreshadowed instability. The republic declared independence on 9 September 1991 and became <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>, soon plunging into civil war.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tajik-ssr.jpg" alt="Tajik SSR" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: SVG by Pianist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Tajik_Soviet_Socialist_Republic_(1953%E2%80%931991),_Flag_of_Tajikistan_(1991%E2%80%931992).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1929 CE – 1991 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Stalinabad</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bobojon Gafurov, Jabbor Rasulov, Rahmon Nabiyev</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (state atheism)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tajik, Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tajik Soviet Socialist Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1960&amp;focus=tajik-ssr" aria-label="Tajik SSR on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6388 794 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tajik SSR lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6708 1050L6708 1049M6708 1050L5820 533M6708 1050L6708 1049"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6708 1049h0M5820 533h0M6708 1049h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6708 1050h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6708 1050h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1960&amp;focus=tajik-ssr">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
